;;; `dead` is NOT upward-closed under `absent`: the probe of a program that
;;; is not monotone (Q-M1f.564).
;;;
;;; ── The premise this refutes ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One could read:
;;;
;;;   dead(X) — X holds a contradiction. Monotone: X ⊆ Y ∧ dead(X) ⇒ dead(Y),
;;;   because the KB is append-only and nothing retracts.
;;;
;;; *Append-only, nothing retracts* makes saturation **inflationary**. It does
;;; not make it **monotone in its input**, and `(absent P)` is exactly what
;;; separates the two — which `docs/ein/reasoning/02-kb-evolution/03-absent-and-naf.md`
;;; § What follows says from the other direction: removing a fact can flip an
;;; absent and *fabricate* a contradiction the full KB never had.
;;;
;;; Here `bad` fires only while `q` is missing: with `(p A)` and `(q A)` the
;;; candidates, a node that holds `(p A)` without `(q A)` matches `bad`, and
;;; one that holds both does not. A search that took a superset of a dead
;;; set for dead would never reach the second.
;;;
;;; ── What is said of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `bad` concludes `(false)` from `(absent (q ?x))` over `q`, which a
;;; commitment can still extend: a site of the static check, so the program
;;; is flagged. It loads, every route takes the cube and none learns from a
;;; dead node, every command answers it with the warning that it is not
;;; monotone, and nothing is claimed of its answer.
;;;
;;; The goal is `is-a` rather than `p`. An answer with an empty goal extent
;;; is a claim like any other, `(model)` the class of a state where the goal
;;; has no row (Q-M1e.13); the goal stays as it was written.
;;;
;;; ── A mis-encoded obligation ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The rule says *a world with p and without q is false* and never says *q
;;; is required*, which is what `(open ?R)` is for (Q-M1d.2): there is no
;;; definition of `q` and `p`, no obligation, only the rule that asserts
;;; `(false)`. A program that means the requirement states it as an
;;; obligation, or concludes from a stored negative as `std.algebra`'s
;;; `total` does, and is monotone.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation is-a T T :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation p T :why "{?3} has p")
(relation q T :why "{?3} has q")

(is-a A T)

(rule bad ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} has p without q")

(query
  :goal   (is-a ?x T))
